1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,660
Episode 39.

3
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:07,500
The Great Paper Airplane Claim.

4
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:10,217
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,217 --> 00:00:15,665
Why was one long flight not enough to prove that Leo's design was the best in the world?

6
00:00:15,665 --> 00:00:17,092
Ready?

7
00:00:17,092 --> 00:00:19,171
Let us find out.

8
00:00:19,171 --> 00:00:23,586
Leo throws a paper airplane across the classroom test area.

9
00:00:23,586 --> 00:00:26,453
It lands beyond Maya's plane.

10
00:00:26,453 --> 00:00:30,448
My design is the best in the world, he announces.

11
00:00:30,448 --> 00:00:34,035
Pip begins preparing a very large trophy.

12
00:00:34,035 --> 00:00:36,494
Maya asks a question.

13
00:00:36,494 --> 00:00:39,388
Did we throw the planes in the same way?

14
00:00:39,388 --> 00:00:41,861
Leo had a running start.

15
00:00:41,861 --> 00:00:44,021
Maya stood still.

16
00:00:44,021 --> 00:00:48,980
The paper was different, and one plane had a paper clip attached.

17
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:52,472
We changed several things, she says.

18
00:00:52,472 --> 00:00:55,774
We cannot tell which one made the difference.

19
00:00:55,774 --> 00:00:59,538
Ms. Park helps them plan a fairer comparison.

20
00:00:59,538 --> 00:01:08,111
They use the same kind of paper, the same starting line, and the same person to launch both designs as consistently as possible.

21
00:01:08,111 --> 00:01:13,097
Everyone stays behind the line while a plane is thrown into the clear area.

22
00:01:13,097 --> 00:01:16,480
They measure several flights for each design.

23
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:19,769
Sometimes Leo's plane travels farther.

24
00:01:19,769 --> 00:01:22,282
Sometimes Maya's does.

25
00:01:22,282 --> 00:01:26,059
Pip lowers the trophy to a more reasonable size.

26
00:01:26,059 --> 00:01:30,108
The children compare the results and notice variation.

27
00:01:30,108 --> 00:01:34,510
One spectacular flight does not represent every attempt.

28
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:37,119
Leo changes his statement.

29
00:01:37,119 --> 00:01:42,894
In our small test, this design usually traveled farther under these conditions.

30
00:01:42,894 --> 00:01:46,086
That is less dramatic, he says.

31
00:01:46,086 --> 00:01:50,611
But it tells people what you actually tested, Maya replies.

32
00:01:50,611 --> 00:01:54,796
Pip presents an award anyway: Best Improved Claim.

33
00:01:54,796 --> 00:01:56,970
Leo accepts it.

34
00:01:56,970 --> 00:02:02,567
Next time, he says, I want to test which plane stays in the air longest.

35
00:02:02,567 --> 00:02:04,633
Maya smiles.

36
00:02:04,633 --> 00:02:08,206
A different question may need a different measurement.

37
00:02:08,206 --> 00:02:11,114
Now, a little more to discover.

38
00:02:11,114 --> 00:02:17,744
A fair comparison tries to keep other important conditions similar while changing the feature being studied.

39
00:02:17,744 --> 00:02:20,842
Repeated trials reveal variation.

40
00:02:20,842 --> 00:02:25,258
A claim should match the evidence and the conditions of the test.

41
00:02:25,258 --> 00:02:30,258
Measuring distance answers a different question from measuring time in the air.

42
00:02:30,258 --> 00:02:33,097
Let us hear the main idea again.

43
00:02:33,097 --> 00:02:37,092
Leo's first claim was much larger than his evidence.

44
00:02:37,092 --> 00:02:43,002
The club controlled more conditions, repeated the flights, and used careful wording.

45
00:02:43,002 --> 00:02:47,581
They also noticed that the meaning of best depends on what they measure.

46
00:02:47,581 --> 00:02:50,557
Here are three useful expressions.

47
00:02:50,557 --> 00:02:53,682
You can listen, or say them with me.

48
00:02:53,682 --> 00:02:56,480
Did we keep the conditions the same?

49
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:59,279
Did we keep the conditions the same?

50
00:02:59,279 --> 00:03:01,970
Let us try it more than once.

51
00:03:01,970 --> 00:03:04,660
Let us try it more than once.

52
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:07,214
What does the evidence support?

53
00:03:07,214 --> 00:03:09,769
What does the evidence support?

54
00:03:09,769 --> 00:03:12,364
Remember our listening question?

55
00:03:12,364 --> 00:03:17,812
Why was one long flight not enough to prove that Leo's design was the best in the world?

56
00:03:17,812 --> 00:03:19,945
Here is the answer.

57
00:03:19,945 --> 00:03:28,396
Other conditions differed, results can vary, and the small test could not compare every design or every meaning of best.

58
00:03:28,396 --> 00:03:32,051
And here is a question just for your imagination.

59
00:03:32,051 --> 00:03:37,255
What everyday claim would you enjoy testing with a simple, fair comparison?

60
00:03:37,255 --> 00:03:40,366
There is more than one possible answer.

61
00:03:40,366 --> 00:03:43,614
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
